# Who owns the edge of each range. One row per answer, with how many claims in the corpus gave it.
#
# It works as a pair with narrowsWhen
#   One says what would make a range smaller; the other says who owns the edge it would have to
#   move. A negotiated bound moves by renegotiating; a bound that is simply where the measurements
#   fell moves by measuring again; an intrinsic one does not move at all. The same width, three
#   different courses of action, and only one of them is a purchase order.
#
# What a required, typed answer shows
#   Because every claim must answer it, two answers stand out that a single optional column could
#   not have produced: `none`, where nothing sets the edge and the range is simply where the
#   measurements fell, and the derivation that points at a sibling element where the model already
#   states the author. Which of the two leads moves with the corpus, so prefer the shape of the
#   answer to its place in the order; run this report for the order.
-- pm:Claim/pm:boundOrigin, over the corpus.
SELECT e.who_owns_the_edge, count(*) AS claims
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/edges.sqlc)
) e
JOIN (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
